// Practice quiz: Grid
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: html-css-js/html-and-css/grid

::o1 fr after fixed tracks::A 900px grid has grid-template-columns 300px 1fr 1fr and no gap. How wide is each 1fr column? {
=300px #Correct. The fixed 300px track comes first; the 600px left over splits into two equal shares.
~450px #Not quite. That splits the whole 900px between the two fr columns, ignoring the fixed track.
~1px #Not quite. fr is a share of the leftover space, not a pixel size.
~225px #Not quite. That divides by four; there are only two fr shares.
}

::o1 minmax and auto-fill::What does grid-template-columns repeat(auto-fill, minmax(150px, 1fr)) do in a 500px container? {
=It makes 3 columns, the largest number of at-least-150px columns that fit, and stretches them to fill #Correct. Three 150px columns fit in 500px but four do not, and the 1fr maximum shares out the rest.
~It makes exactly one 150px column #Not quite. auto-fill repeats the track as many times as fit.
~It makes columns of exactly 150px and leaves 50px empty #Not quite. The 1fr maximum lets the columns grow to fill the width.
~It makes 4 columns that overflow #Not quite. auto-fill never creates more columns than fit at their minimum.
}

::o2 rectangular areas::Which grid-template-areas value does the browser reject? {
=a a then a b, written "a a" "a b" #Correct. Area a would be an L-shape, and every named area must be one filled rectangle, so the declaration is invalid.
~"a a" "b b" #Not quite. Both areas are rectangles of two cells.
~"a b" "a b" #Not quite. Both areas are vertical rectangles.
~"a ." "b b" #Not quite. The dot is an empty cell, and both named areas are rectangles.
}

::o2 placing an item::The grid has grid-template-areas "head head" "nav main". How does the nav element go into the nav area? {
=Give the nav element grid-area nav #Correct. A child takes a named area with the grid-area property.
~Give the nav element the class nav #Not quite. Class names do not place items; grid-area does.
~Write the nav element first in the HTML #Not quite. Source order fills cells only when no area is named.
~Add nav to grid-template-columns #Not quite. grid-template-columns sizes tracks; it does not place items.
}

::o3 one line of items::A toolbar holds five buttons of different widths in a single row with space between groups. Which layout model fits best, and why? {
=Flexbox, because it is one line of content-sized items #Correct. Flexbox is single-axis; nothing has to line up in a second direction.
~Grid, because there are more than three items #Not quite. The number of items does not decide it; the dimensions do.
~Grid, because Grid always replaces Flexbox #Not quite. Each solves a different problem; a single row is Flexbox's job.
~Neither, because buttons cannot be laid out #Not quite. Buttons are ordinary flex or grid items.
}
